preConditionHook:
// THE BAR THAT ENDS A PULLBACK INSIDE AN EXISTING BULL LEG. Three things at
// once, at three different lags, which is why the composite is carried whole:
//
//   1. The PRIOR bar closed DOWN -- the pullback exists (close[1] < open[1]).
//   2. THIS bar makes a higher high than it -- the pullback is over.
//   3. THIS bar is itself a strong bull reversal bar.
//
// plus the CONTEXT term close[0] > close[pullbackLookback], which is the
// catalog's Momentum(pullbackLookback) carried inside the rule. It is kept
// rather than left to a pane because without it the rule fires on every
// pullback in a downtrend as well; a placement that wants the leg read
// differently can still AND its own trend filter on top.
//
// This is the source's high-1 proxy WITHOUT the leg counter -- the
// mechanizable reading -- so it does not belong to the leg-counting family and
// does not inline HighLowBarCount's counter.
//
// REVERSAL BAR SHAPE is the corpus's strong-trend-bar shape (StrongTrendBar,
// TrendDurationBars): a strict body direction, a body of at least bodyFraction
// of the bar's own range, and BOTH tails no more than tailFraction of it.
//
// Both sides are computed in the hook so the shape test is written once, and
// the locals are class members reassigned every bar rather than accumulated.
//
// pullbackLookback is a double input used as a subscript; the narrowing is
// deliberate, as README "Input variables" spells out.
//
// Products, not quotients: EasyLanguage raises a runtime divide-by-zero and a
// zero-range bar is legal data.
//
// Deepest bar touched: pullbackLookback.
rng = high[0] - low[0];
bullShape = el_gt(close[0], open[0]) && el_ge(close[0] - open[0], bodyFraction * rng) && el_le(high[0] - close[0], tailFraction * rng) && el_le(open[0] - low[0], tailFraction * rng);
bearShape = el_lt(close[0], open[0]) && el_ge(open[0] - close[0], bodyFraction * rng) && el_le(close[0] - low[0], tailFraction * rng) && el_le(high[0] - open[0], tailFraction * rng);
